<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qalawun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1222–<a href="/years/1290">1290</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qalawun was a sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> in the late thirteenth century and founder of a dynasty that ruled for about a century. His campaigns continued the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. His descendants included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a>, under whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> power reached its height.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qalawun.jpg" alt="Qalawun" width="270" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ahmed zakaria 2025 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%82%D8%A8%D8%A9_%D8%B6%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%AD_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%84%D8%B7%D8%A7%D9%86_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%85%D9%84%D9%88%D9%83%D9%8A_%D9%82%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%88%D9%88%D9%86.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Qalawun was a Kipchak of the Burj Oghli tribe, the same tribe as the earlier sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a>. Both were probably sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> during the Mongol invasions of Kipchak territories in the 1220s and 1230s. At fourteen he was brought by slave merchants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, then under Ayyubid rule, and bought as a mamluk by an amir whom the sources name differently. The price, a thousand dinars, was unusually high and earned him the nickname al-Alfi, the Thousander.</p><p>He spoke little Arabic at first but rose to become an emir under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a>, whose son al-Said Barakah married Qalawun's daughter. Baybars died in 1277 and Barakah succeeded him. In early 1279 a revolt forced Barakah to abdicate, and his seven-year-old brother Solamish was placed on the throne with Qalawun as atabeg, the true holder of power. Qalawun argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> needed an adult ruler, sent Solamish into exile in late 1279 and took the title al-Malik al-Mansur, the victorious king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>The governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, Sunqur al-Ashqar, declared himself sultan, but Qalawun defeated him in 1280. In 1281 the two reconciled when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and together they repelled him at the Second Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>. Barakah died in exile at al-Karak in 1280, and rumour held that Qalawun had poisoned him.</p><p>Like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a>, Qalawun made treaties with the remaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a>, military orders and lords, always on terms favourable to himself. His treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> forbade new fortifications and let him collect half the city's taxes. In 1281 he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> against Charles I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, and in 1290 he made trade agreements with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Despite the treaties he took the Hospitaller fortress of Margat in 1285, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latakia">Latakia</a> in 1287 and Tripoli on 27 April 1289, ending the County of Tripoli.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1290 reinforcements for King Henry arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> and slaughtered merchants and peasants, Christians and Muslims alike. Qalawun demanded the murderers and received neither an explanation nor the men, so he judged the ten-year truce of 1284 broken and besieged the city. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> on 10 November 1290, and his son al-Ashraf Khalil took Acre the next year. Khalil was assassinated in 1293, after which Qalawun's younger son an-Nasir Muhammad claimed power, continuing the Qalawunid line that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for over a century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qalawun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Baibars summoned Het'um to change allegiance in 1266 and the king went to the Il-Khan for help, Al-Mansur Ali and <b>Qalawun</b> overran the outnumbered Armenians at the Disaster of Mari, killing Prince T'oros, capturing Levon, sacking and burning Sis, massacring thousands and carrying off 40,000 captives.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Campaigns under Baybars, <b>Qalawun</b> and al-Nasir Muhammad secured Syria and the Hejaz, reduced Makuria in Nubia to vassalage and raided Cilician Armenia, while the victory at Ain Jalut in 1260 and later at Homs and Marj al-Saffar checked Mongol expansion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Some sultans founded dynasties, and <b>Qalawun</b>’s descendants held the throne for most of a century, but the senior amirs often set up a sultan’s young son as a figurehead, and usurpations and murders were common.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mamluk-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Mamluk Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mamluk sultan <b>Qalawun</b> stormed Tripoli in April 1289, and a new city was built inland.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-tripoli">County of Tripoli</a></li><li><blockquote>Adur later corresponded with Sultan <b>Qalawun</b>, offering alliance against Makuria.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-al-abwab">Kingdom of al-Abwab</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1266" aria-label="Cairo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5361 1067 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Qalawun"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5811 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5811 1337h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1266">What was here in 1266? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
